+## Publication and recovery contract
+
+Keep `ash/post/` an ordinary directory with the existing filenames. Preserve
+unrelated content and supported access restrictions. Compute, validate, and
+close all five staged parquets before replacing any canonical parquet. Use
+non-catalogable temporary names on the destination filesystem and protect
+staging access. Qualify the existing owned ParquetSink/commit_staged machinery
+before reuse; do not introduce a parallel storage-generation architecture.
+
+Each canonical file is replaced atomically. Multi-file snapshot consistency is
+not promised: readers may observe complete files from different executions
+across independent opens. There is no single transaction spanning parquet,
+version manifest, README, NoDb mappings, and catalog. Native success requires
+all five replacements confirmed; Python success retains all existing downstream
+persistence/documentation/catalog steps and their error propagation.
+
+Failure before publication leaves files present at native entry unchanged.
+Attempt backup/restore rollback after ordinary handled publication failures,
+and verify/report whether restoration completed. A rename error on NFS can be
+ambiguous; reconcile destination identities, including the failing target, or
+report an indeterminate outcome. Failed restoration, process termination, or
+unverifiable storage outcomes may leave a mixed set of complete files (or a
+subset on first publication) and retained recovery files. Preserve recovery
+evidence. Detected publication or restoration failures propagate as job failures;
+abrupt worker termination retains existing RQ failure handling. Prove complete
+regeneration on retry.
+Do not claim failure atomicity for server failure, SIGKILL, or rollback failure.
+
+After all five replacements are confirmed, cleanup failure is observable but
+does not retroactively mean that publication rolled back. Define private-stage
+permissions, supported ownership/group/mode/ACL handling, commit point, and
+recovery-file ownership before implementation. Never silently weaken access or
+delete the only recovery copy to satisfy a no-temporary-files assertion.
+
+Existing producer cleanup and major-version invalidation remain separate Python
+policy. Preservation applies after those policies, to files at native entry.
+Validate and anchor input and destination boundaries before producer cleanup
+or major-version invalidation; reject hostile path substitutions without affecting external
+sentinels. Pure pathname checks followed by unanchored reopening are insufficient.
+
+## Preimplementation contract checkpoint
+
+Before production edits, ratify an exact native signature, required and optional
+columns per input/model, telemetry/no-data shape, metadata representation, and
+null/NaN/infinity/key/area/class policies. Define publication ownership from
+acquisition through release, participating writers, and how to detect/respond to
+destination or input changes before replacement. Primitive locking success does
+not establish concurrent transaction isolation. Characterize absent files separately
+from zero-row files, and optional hydrology absent/empty/partial/unmatched cases.
+Freeze float32 area semantics, annual hillslope-day area sums, calendar-date
+collapse, cumulative selection by year0/julian, and sequential return-period tie
+ordering. Do not correct historical formulas during extraction.
+
+Promote the accepted publication, native-boundary, and scheduling rules into
+current canonical WEPPpy contracts. Obtain two independent contract reviews,
+resolve medium/high findings, and commit the accepted checkpoint as an ancestor
+before implementation. The prior native-contract.md is a preliminary proposal,
+not a completed checkpoint. No additional external dependency is assumed.
+
